# PROSPECTIVE summary scaffold, generated by scripts/new-provenance-summary.sh.
# TODO: replace the placeholder summaryText/decisionPoint below with the
# actual distilled reasoning behind this PR before committing — see
# docs/plans/TRANSCRIPT_SUMMARY_GUIDELINES.md. Timestamps below are real
# (from `git log main..HEAD` on branch fix/535-remove-quad-incremental); everything else that
# isn't marked TODO can be left as-is.

ghpull:538 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "dag_rdf: point-remove QuadTable entries instead of rebuilding all indexes" ;
    bl:number 538 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:535 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr538 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:535 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-08-17T11:32:52Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-08-17T11:34:04Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr538Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #535: QuadTable::remove_quad rebuilt all five indexes (subject_predicate, object_predicate, predicate, triple_id, four_keys) from scratch on every single-quad delete, an O(total store size) cost paid once per triple in a SPARQL DELETE DATA. Read quadtable.rs fully first to understand each index's exact shape before touching anything, then traced every remove_quad caller (datalog::incremental's apply_deletions/forward_phase, sparql_update.rs) to confirm removes always complete before any delta-range snapshot is taken elsewhere (e.g. reasoner.rs's quad_list[delta_start..delta_end] slicing, truncate_to's append-only assumption) -- meaning remove_quad is free to reorder quad_list internally without breaking those invariants. That let point removal use a swap-based approach: four_keys_index changed from HashSet<Quad> to HashMap<Quad, QuadListIndex> so remove_quad can find a quad's own position without scanning quad_list; the target's entries are removed directly from each index; quad_list is compacted by moving the previously-last quad into the freed slot (not a shift-preserving Vec::remove); and only that relocated quad's four index entries are fixed up. Cost is now bounded by the small index buckets involved, not total store size. Wrote unit tests first (ignored) covering index consistency across a shared bucket and the relocated quad, no-op on a missing quad including repeated calls, and the single-remaining-quad edge case, then implemented and un-ignored them per this repo's TDD workflow." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:538 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr538 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr538Decision .

session:pr538Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Chose swap-removal (move the last quad_list element into the removed slot) over an order-preserving Vec::remove for compacting quad_list after point-removing a quad's index entries. Swap-removal only requires fixing up the one relocated quad's four index entries (O(bucket size)); an order-preserving remove would still need every subsequent quad's stored QuadListIndex decremented across all four index structures, which is O(total index entries) and would not actually fix the asymptotic problem the issue was filed about." ;
    agp:alternative "Order-preserving Vec::remove (shift every element after the removed position down by one) was rejected: it preserves get_all_quads' documented insertion-order guarantee exactly, but re-adds an O(total store size) cost on every delete via the necessary index-position renumbering, defeating the point of the fix. Confirmed via grep that no caller depends on insertion order surviving a delete (datalog::reasoner's delta-range slicing and datalog::incremental's truncate_to only ever combine appends with position snapshots taken after all removes in a given operation complete)." .
